'''Trapper Wolf''' was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] [[New Republic pilot|pilot]] and [[New Republic officer|officer]] for the [[New Republic]]. He flew an [[T-65B X-wing starfighter]] as leader of an attack [[squadron]] with [[pilot]]s [[Sash Ketter]] and [[Jib Dodger]]. Circa [[9 ABY]], the trio located the signal from a [[Homing beacon|tracking beacon]] that had originally been activated during the [[Bothan-Five incident|''Bothan-Five'' incident]], a [[mercenary]] attack on a [[New Republic Correctional Transport]]. Wolf and the other pilots detected the tracker's signal on board a [[Roost|space station]] managed by the criminal [[Ranzar Malk]]. After seeing a [[gunship]] preparing to launch from within the [[space station]] as they approached, the trio attacked the station in their [[starfighter]]s.

Sometime later, Wolf and [[Captain]] [[Carson Teva]] approached the gunship ''[[Razor Crest]]'' during a patrol, which did not have an emitting [[transponder]]. After the [[starship]]'s pilot, the [[Mandalorian]] [[Din Djarin]], sent a ping to the pair, Wolf privately discussed with Teva, connecting the Mandalorian to the prisoner breakout during the ''Bothan-Five'' incident. Djarin tried to flee from the New Republic pilots, eventually escaping their sights at canyons of the [[planet]] [[Maldo Kreis]]. The two pilots then checked records on the ''Crest'', they learned of selfless actions Djarin had committed. The pair soon rescued the gunship from a horde of [[knobby white ice spider]]s and let the Mandalorian go.

==Biography==
===Attack on the Roost===
{{Quote|Yeah, I got a clear signal on the tracking beacon.''"<br />"''Copy that.|Trapper Wolf and Jib Dodger|<ref name="Chapter 6" />}}
A veteran of the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]],<ref name="Hasbro" /> Trapper Wolf eventually served as a [[New Republic pilot|pilot]] in the [[New Republic Starfighter Corps]] during the [[New Republic Era|time]] of the [[New Republic]], flying a [[T-65B X-wing starfighter]].<ref name="Chapter 6" /> By [[9 ABY]],<ref name="Date">{{MandoDate}}</ref> Wolf was a respected member<ref name="Comp Encyclopedia">''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]''</ref> and leader of a New Republic attack squadron<ref name="Connections">{{DisneyGallery|Connections}}</ref> identified as the [[Adelphi Rangers]].<ref name="Chapter 10" /> The squadron was tasked with patrolling the outer reaches of [[the galaxy]],<ref name="Hasbro" /> with Wolf often flying alongside fellow Adelphi [[pilot]]s [[Jib Dodger]] and [[Sash Ketter]].<ref name="Comp Encyclopedia" /> Their duties included locating certain active New Republic [[Homing beacon|tracking beacons]]. One such beacon was activated aboard the ''[[Bothan-5]]'', a [[New Republic Correctional Transport]], during a [[Bothan-Five incident|raid]] by several [[Mercenary|mercenaries]]; however, before any attack squadron could reach the vessel, the beacon was transported off the [[starship]] on the [[gunship]] ''[[Razor Crest]]'' and moved to the [[Roost]], a [[space station]] controlled by the criminal [[Ranzar Malk]].<ref name="Chapter 6" />

[[File:X-WingsAttackingStation-Chapter6.png|thumb|left|300px|Wolf, Dodger, and Ketter starting their attack run on Malk's space station.]]
Piloting [[T-65B X-wing starfighter]]s, Wolf and his squadron exited [[hyperspace]] near the [[Space station|station]]. As they passed the ''Razor Crest'', which had just left the station, Wolf confirmed that the beacon's signal was definitely coming from within the station. [[Gender|He]] then noted that there seemed to be another gunship preparing to launch from the station, which had been preparing to destroy the ''Razor Crest''; however, the New Republic pilots took it as reason to attack. Opening fire with their [[laser cannon]]s, the three pilots destroyed the gunship in the station's hangar and then proceeded to circle round and fire upon the exterior of the station itself,<ref name="Chapter 6" /> destroying the Roost.<ref name="Jib card">{{Topps|set=2023 Topps Star Wars|cardname=Jib Dodger|link=https://www.tcdb.com/ViewCard.cfm/sid/370777/cid/22170344}}</ref>

===Patrol in the Arkanis sector===
====Pursuing the Mandalorian====
{{Quote|We got a runner.''"<br />"''I'm on it.|Trapper Wolf and Carson Teva, as the ''Razor Crest'' attempted to flee|<ref name="Chapter 10" />}}
[[File:RazorCrestFlanked-TMCh10.png|thumb|right|300px|Wolf and Teva flank either side of the ''Razor Crest'']]
Sometime later, Wolf and fellow pilot [[Captain]] [[Carson Teva]] were sweeping<ref name="Chapter 10" /> the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim's]] [[Arkanis sector]]<ref name="Arkanis sector">In "[[Chapter 10: The Passenger]]," [[Din Djarin]] encounters Trapper Wolf and [[Carson Teva]] while travelling between [[Tatooine]] and [[Trask]] of the [[Kol Iben system]]. [[Peli Motto]] states that the [[Kol Iben]] [[Star system|system]] is within the same [[sector]] as Tatooine, which ''[[Ultimate Star Wars]]'' places in the [[Arkanis sector]] of the [[Outer Rim Territories]].</ref> for Imperial holdouts when they encountered ''Razor Crest'', which had no emitting [[transponder]], near the<ref name="Chapter 10" /> [[planet]]<ref name="Chapter 1">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 1: The Mandalorian}}</ref> [[Maldo Kreis]]. The pair flanked either side of the gunship while Teva reached out to the vessel over a comm channel. The ''Crest''{{'s}} pilot, the [[Mandalorian]] [[bounty hunter]] [[Din Djarin]], replied, and Teva questioned the absence of a transponder signal. After Djarin and the captain cleared up need to run a beacon, Teva asked the Mandalorian to send a ping, which the latter eventually did. Having received this, Wolf realized that Djarin's ship was connected with the raid aboard ''Bothan-Five'', contacting Teva to discuss the matter on channel two.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

After talking about the ''Razor Crest''{{'s}} possible involvement in the ''Bothan-Five'' heist, the New Republic pilots unlocked their [[X-wing starfighter]]'s s-foils and Teva asked Djarin if his ship had been in proximity with ''Bothan-Five''. Djarin then attempted to flee the pair aboard the ''Crest'' towards Maldo Kreis. Wolf was the first to acknowledge this, with Teva moving in pursuit of the Mandalorian after. The two pursued Djarin through the planet's [[atmosphere]], Wolf wondering where the ''Razor Crest'' was heading. The pair then followed the gunship through a canyon, but lost sight of Djarin's ship. Teva told Wolf to head north to cover more ground.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

====A change of heart====
{{Quote|We ran the tabs on the ''Razor Crest''. You have an arrest warrant for the abduction of prisoner X-Six-Nine-Eleven.|Carson Teva explains to Din Djarin what he and Trapper Wolf learned about the Mandalorian|<ref name="Chapter 10" />}}
[[File:WolfMaldoKreis.png|thumb|left|300px|Trapper Wolf on Maldo Kreis.]]
Wolf and Teva ran tabs on the ''Razor Crest'', discovering that Djarin had an arrest warrant for his involvement in the raid on ''Bothan-Five''. They also learned from ''Bothan-Five''{{'s}} security records that the Mandalorian had helped capture three other mercenaries involved, as well as attempting to preserve the life of [[Lieutenant]] [[Lant Davan]] of the [[New Republic Correctional Corps]]. The pair eventually discovered the ''Razor Crest'' damaged and being swarmed by [[knobby white ice spider]]s in a [[cave]] under the surface of Maldo Kreis. Wolf and Teva fired on the creatures with their X-wings before landing and firing on the smaller ones from their cockpits using [[A280 blaster rifle]]s.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

Djarin exited his damaged gunship, and Teva explained what he and Wolf had learned about the Mandalorian, revealing that the pair were letting the bounty hunter go. When Djarin sought help repairing the ''Crest''{{'s}} [[hull]], Wolf looked to the captain, who instead suggested the Mandalorian fix his transponder so the two New Republic pilots would not have to vaporize him during their next patrol. The pair reentered their cockpits, departing the cave on their X-wings.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

Wolf was later present in [[Adelphi Base]], speaking with Dodger and Ketter, while Teva got a message from [[Greef Karga]] asking for help with dealing with [[pirate]]s on [[Nevarro]].<ref name="Chapter 21">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 21: The Pirate}}</ref>

==Personality and traits==
{{Quote|I don't know where he thinks he's goin' in that thing.''"<br />"''That thing's gonna break apart in this atmosphere.|Trapper Wolf and Carson Teva, on the ''Razor Crest''|<ref name="Chapter 10" />}}
Trapper Wolf was a [[human]] male who had fair skin, [[Color|black]] [[hair]], and brown [[eye]]s.<ref name="Chapter 6" /> After learning of Din Djarin's selfless actions aboard ''Bothan-Five'', he and Carson Teva chose to let the Mandalorian go. When Djarin asked for help fixing the ''Razor Crest''{{'s}} hull, Wolf looked to Teva for their answer to the Mandalorian.<ref name="Chapter 10" />

==Equipment==
{{Quote|Carson, can you switch over to channel two?''"<br />"''Copy.|Trapper Wolf and Carson Teva|<ref name="Chapter 10" />}}
Wolf wore an orange and white [[flight suit]]. During his squadron's attack on Ranzar Malk's space station, he wore a white [[K-22995 light flight helmet]] with blue and yellow markings and two New Republic roundels,<ref name="Chapter 6" /> but later adopted a gray helmet with black and yellow markings bearing the emblem of the [[Wolfpack]] during his pursuit of the ''Razor Crest''. Wolf was also capable with an A280 [[blaster rifle]].<ref name="Chapter 10" /> When not on duty, Wolf wore a [[hat]] and a leather jacket, which was outfitted with his [[Rebel command insignia|command insignia]] with five [[Color|blue]] pips.<ref name="Chapter 24">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 24: The Return}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
===Cameo===
{{Quote|In ''The Mandalorian'', we have a few X-wing pilots. One of them is Deb Chow, who you mentioned. One of them is Dave Filoni, and one's Rick Famuyiwa. It looks like something from the '70s, that sequence.|Jon Favreau, on Filoni's role|<ref name="Connections" />}}
Trapper Wolf first appeared in "[[Chapter 6: The Prisoner]]" of [[Jon Favreau]]'s [[Disney+]] television series ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'', which was directed by [[Rick Famuyiwa]]<ref name="Chapter 6" /> and aired on [[December 13]] [[2019]].<ref name="DTCI">{{WebCite|url=https://lumiere-a.akamaihd.net/v1/documents/the_mandalorian_project_one-sheet_10_ec1e005f.pdf|text=The Mandalorian Media Kit|work=[https://dtcimedia.disney.com Disney Direct-to-Consumer & International]|archivedate=20230315011507}}</ref> The pilot was portrayed by producer [[Dave Filoni]], who made his cameo appearance alongside<ref name="Chapter 6" /> fellow series directors<ref name="Blog">{{SW|url=news/the-mandalorian-revealed|text=''The Mandalorian'' First Image, Directors Revealed}}</ref> Famuyiwa and [[Deborah Chow]], who portrayed the other two New Republic pilots. The character's name is shared with<ref name="Chapter 6" /> the real world animal, the [[Wikipedia:Wolf|wolf]], of which Filoni himself is a fan.<ref name="Ahsoka's Untold Tales">{{SWYouTube|lS4sclln5J4|Ahsoka's Untold Tales Panel &#124; Star Wars Celebration Europe 2016}}</ref> Filoni teased his role in the show prior to the episode's air date in an interview with [[Andi Gutierrez]] hosted on the official ''[[Star Wars]]'' YouTube channel.<ref name="Cameo Tease">{{StarWarsShow|uE2YgHf7ZuA|Dave Filoni Talks The Mandalorian, And Disney+ Is Now Live!|sw_url=video/dave-filoni-talks-the-mandalorian-and-disney-is-now-live}}</ref>

As his debut acting role, Filoni portrayed Wolf at Favreau's suggestion, and had Famuyiwa and Chow portray the other pilots as Filoni did not feel comfortable acting alone.<ref name="Entertainment-Tonight">{{OfficialYouTube|channel=@EntertainmentTonight|name=Entertainment Tonight|link=Wikipedia:Entertainment Tonight|video=hyOYs_OIMuM|text=Star Wars: Dave Filoni on THAT Clone Wars Finale and Mandalorian Season 2 &#124; Full Interview|quote=ET's Ash Crossan spoke with 'Star Wars: The Clone Wars' and 'The Mandalorian' executive producer, Dave Filoni. The 'Clone Wars' series finale drops May 4 on Disney+, the same day as the premiere of the behind-the-scenes docu-series 'Disney Gallery: The Mandalorian.'|archivedate=20200505115327}}</ref> The idea, according to Filoni, had started as a joke which Favreau liked. The three directors continuously messaged each other about the shoot during the prior week, questioning their decisions to take part, but Favreau was reluctant to let the trio bail.<ref name="Connections" />

===Portrayal===
{{Quote|The direction for me was, 'Act disinterested and bored.' And I was like, 'I can do that.'|Dave Filoni|<ref name="Connections" />}}
Before filming the "X-wing pilot shoot," Filoni did second unit shooting for Famuyiwa's "[[Chapter 2: The Child]]" that same day filming the sequence at the [[mudhorn cave]], where the prospect of filming as an X-wing pilot loomed over the producer. He considered using a scheduled flight he had as an excuse, but was convinced otherwise by Favreau and felt he could learn from having an acting role.<ref name="Connections" /> When the shooting took place, Favreau gave Filoni the character's dialogue during filming, and told him to "be boring"<ref name="Entertainment-Tonight" /> and act disinterested in order to relate with the calm impressions given by pilots in the real world or in films like [[Wikipedia:Apocalypse Now|''Apocalypse Now'']].<ref name="Connections" />

In filming the scene, Filoni and the other two directors were filmed inside the [[T-70 X-wing starfighter]] built for the [[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge|''Star Wars'': Galaxy's Edge]] theme park in [[Wikipedia:Orlando|Orlando]], [[Wikipedia:United States|USA]]. The life-size X-wing model was altered to allow the trio to sit inside for their shots. The X-wing was elevated on a platform to recreate the turbulence on the starfighter. The final cut of Filoni's shots were made to look like the scenes of the X-wing pilots in their cockpits in [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''Star Wars:'' Episode IV ''A New Hope'']]. When talking about the "X-wing pilot shoot" on ''[[Disney Gallery: The Mandalorian]]'', Filoni acknowledged that he had indeed gained insight in preparing for and being filmed.<ref name="Connections" />

Filoni later reappeared in the second season<ref name="Chapter 10" /> and the third season of the series.<ref name="Chapter 21" /> In the third season he wore a hat, which was different than his trademark hat, and was specifically brought in for his scene.<ref name="Paul QnA">{{YouTube|video=46pR7EOJ7Jo|text=Unboxing The Mandalorian Chapter 21: The Pirate. Funboxing Sundays!|channel=@Bitterasiandude|name=Bitterasiandude Inc.|date=2023-4-2|archiveurl=https://archive.is/JHsxU}}</ref> In that scene he can also be seen wearing a flight jacket with a five blue-doted [[Rebel_command_insignia#Rank_insignia|rank plaque]].<ref name="Chapter 21" />

